  "name": "Question 12",
  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040800/assets/887bc91e2565fa24630af4cb74b28723783e31f4769495f0a28a622d73d176de.png)\n\nThe thin rod above has the mass density function $\\lambda (x)=x+{x}^{2}.$ Given that the mass of the rod is $\\frac{5}{6}$ units, what is the $x$-coordinate of its center of mass?",
  "worked_solution": "The center of mass of a rod placed along the $x$-axis over the interval $a\\le x\\le b$ whose mass density function is $\\lambda (x)$ is given by \n\n$$\n\\overset{\u2015}{x}=\\frac{{M}_{O}}{m},\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere \n\n- $m={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\lambda (x)\\,\\text{d}x$ is the mass of the rod,\n\n- ${M}_{O}={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\,x\\lambda (x)\\,\\text{d}x$ is the moment of the rod about the origin $O.$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\lambda (x)=x+{x}^{2},\\,a=0,\\,b=1.\n$$\n\nSo,  the moment of the rod about the origin is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{M}_{O} & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}x\\cdot (x+{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{x}^{2}+{x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3}+\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{4}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}+\\frac{1}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{12}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\overset{\u2015}{x}=\\frac{{M}_{O}}{m}=\\frac{(\\frac{7}{12})}{(\\frac{5}{6})}=\\frac{7}{10}.\n$$",
